],"state":{"t":29,"text":"Cattle drives from Texas reach Kansas City. The stockyards are full and the saloons are fuller."}},"log":[{"t":0,"kind":"story","text":"The war is over. Not for Jesse James. You are back on the farm in Clay County with Silas James, Lewis Ballard, Wiley Miller, and none of you means to plough."},{"t":1,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"The Radical legislature keeps the Ironclad Oath: no man who aided the rebellion may vote, preach, teach or practise law. Half of Little Dixie cannot vote."},{"t":1,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,387 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":2,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Camp meetings along the Fishing River draw a thousand souls."},{"t":2,"kind":"gang","text":"Lafe Dalton rides with you now."},{"t":2,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $7,208 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":3,"kind":"gang","text":"Samuel Hughes rides with you now."},{"t":3,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,246 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":4,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Wheat brings 0.85 a bushel in St. Louis this season."},{"t":4,"kind":"gang","text":"Cole Younger rides with you now."},{"t":4,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $5,325 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":6,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Work begins on a railroad bridge over the Missouri at Kansas City. The town that gets the bridge gets the West."},{"t":6,"kind":"job","text":"ST. JOSEPH: BOLD ROBBERY — $1,918 Carried Off"},{"t":7,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"A hard frost in the river counties. The tobacco is in early."},{"t":8,"kind":"story","text":"You marry Belle Hendricks. Your mother cries, the preacher does not ask questions, and your gang gets drunk."},{"t":8,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: A QUIET ROBBERY — $2,335 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":9,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $2,467 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":10,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Grant is elected President. Missouri goes Republican, with most of its rebels barred from the polls."},{"t":10,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: CASHIER MURDERED — Bandits Escape With $1,286"},{"t":11,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $6,258 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":12,"kind":"story","text":"A Jefferson City lawyer takes your money and your case. He knows the governor's friends."},{"t":12,"kind":"job","text":"ST. JOSEPH: A QUIET ROBBERY — $8,303 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":13,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"The Hannibal Bridge opens at Kansas City, the first across the Missouri. Kansas City will outgrow St. Joseph now."},{"t":13,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,896 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":14,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Camp meetings along the Fishing River draw a thousand souls."},{"t":14,"kind":"good","text":"In Lafayette they will say you paid a widow's mortgage. Sympathy +8."},{"t":15,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,662 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":16,"kind":"history","text":"John Newman Edwards of the Kansas City Times, who rode with Jo Shelby, has started printing letters from Missouri's \"outlaws\" and defending them as Southern men driven to it. He will print yours. (After a job you can write to him: legend and Southern sympathy rise, and so does the reward.)","title":"A friend at the Kansas City Times"},{"t":16,"kind":"news","text":"John Newman Edwards prints your letter: you are a Southern man wronged by Radicals, and you name the men who can swear where you were that day."},{"t":17,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Cattle drives from Texas reach Kansas City. The stockyards are full and the saloons are fuller."},{"t":17,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $5,582 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":18,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"The Liberal Republicans and the Democrats carry the state for Gratz Brown. The test oath is struck down; ex-Confederates vote again."},{"t":18,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":18,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $4,895 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":19,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Wheat brings 1.00 a bushel in St. Louis this season."},{"t":19,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":19,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: A QUIET ROBBERY — $3,761 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":20,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":20,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $8,741 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":21,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":21,"kind":"job","text":"LEXINGTON: CASHIER MURDERED — Bandits Escape With $1,734"},{"t":22,"kind":"good","text":"In Ray they will say you paid a widow's mortgage. Sympathy +8."},{"t":23,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: CITIZEN KILLED IN DARING ROBBERY"},{"t":24,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"County courts across the state are fighting the railroad bonds they voted in the boom. In Cass County a mob has killed the officials who sold theirs."},{"t":24,"kind":"good","text":"In Lafayette they will say you paid a widow's mortgage. Sympathy +8."},{"t":25,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"The county fair at Liberty: horse races, a balloon ascension and a fist fight."},{"t":25,"kind":"job","text":"KANSAS CITY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $7,304 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":26,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"The Kansas City Industrial Exposition draws thirty thousand people. Someone robs the gate."},{"t":26,"kind":"news","text":"John Newman Edwards prints your letter: you are a Southern man wronged by Radicals, and you name the men who can swear where you were that day."},{"t":26,"kind":"job","text":"LIBERTY: A QUIET ROBBERY — $5,921 Taken Without a Shot"},{"t":27,"kind":"news","text":"Another letter in the Kansas City Times. Even friends say you should stop writing to the papers."},{"t":27,"kind":"job","text":"RICHMOND: BOLD ROBBERY — $1,739 Carried Off"},{"t":28,"kind":"history","text":"The railroads are across Missouri now and the express companies ship cash in locked safes in the baggage cars. A train can be stopped like a stage, if you pull up a rail. Train jobs are open.","title":"The express cars"},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Silas James takes his stake home, on the horse and with the guns you bought him."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Lewis Ballard takes his stake home, on the horse and with the guns you bought him."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Wiley Miller takes his stake home, on the horse and with the guns you bought him."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Lafe Dalton takes his stake home, on the horse and with the guns you bought him."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Samuel Hughes takes his stake home, on the horse and with the guns you bought him."},{"t":28,"kind":"gang","text":"Cole Younger takes his stake home, on the horse and with the guns you bought him."},{"t":28,"kind":"story","text":"You buy a farm in Tennessee under another name. Nobody there has heard of you."},{"t":29,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Cattle drives from Texas reach Kansas City. The stockyards are full and the saloons are fuller."},{"t":29,"kind":"story","text":"Mr. Howard opens a business in Nashville. He pays his bills on time."},{"t":30,"kind":"state","title":"The State","text":"Panic. Jay Cooke & Company has failed in Philadelphia and banks are closing from New York to St. Louis. Country banks hold less cash than they did (bank cash x0.7 through 1874)."}],"stateLine":{"t":30,"text":"Panic. Jay Cooke & Company has failed in Philadelphia and banks are closing from New York to St. Louis. Country banks hold less cash than they did (bank cash x0.7 through 1874)."}}